# =====================================================================================
# WINDOWS BATCH FILES MUST BE CRLF. This is not style - cmd.exe MIS-PARSES an LF-only
# .cmd file: it drops leading characters on following lines, so REM becomes M, echo
# becomes cho and python becomes thon. The file looks perfect in any editor and fails
# the moment it is double-clicked.
#
# Measured 2026-09-16: START-PRODUCTION-RUN.cmd had been LF-only since the commit that
# created it (10a404a). A run on this laptop produced 20 "'M' is not recognized" errors
# and then "STOPPED - preflight did not pass" - because the line that runs preflight had
# been eaten too. RUN_LOG Finding 137 records this launcher as "tested by running it and
# cancelling at the prompt"; that claim does not survive re-testing.
#
# `eol=crlf` forces CRLF ON CHECKOUT REGARDLESS OF THE MACHINE'S core.autocrlf. That is
# the point: this laptop has autocrlf=true, but the office desktop's git config is not
# something we control or have seen, and "it works if their git is configured like mine"
# is not a guarantee - it is a coin toss on launch morning.
# =====================================================================================
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Everything else: normalised to LF in the repository, native on checkout.
*.py   text
*.md   text
*.sql  text
*.yaml text
*.yml  text
*.csv  text

# Never touch these.
*.xlsx binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
